0 @N01@ SNOTE
1 TEXT The family came to Haworth on 25 February 1820, when Patrick became its perpetual curate, and the Parsonage was their home from then on. Patrick lived there until his death in 1861. The house is now the Brontë Parsonage Museum, on Church Street, Haworth, run by the Brontë Society.
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 NOV 2026
3 TIME 18:10:38Z
2 NOTE MCP: create_shared_note
0 @N02@ SNOTE
1 TEXT His name was written in several ways before he settled on Brontë. The family's parish register has Brunty; his father is also recorded as Prunty; the Cambridge admission list has Branty. See research note N11.
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 NOV 2026
3 TIME 18:10:38Z
2 NOTE MCP: create_shared_note
0 @N03@ SNOTE
1 TEXT Gaskell was shown nine letters Maria wrote to Patrick while they were engaged in 1812. They are the main evidence for her character in the early biographies.
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 NOV 2026
3 TIME 18:10:38Z
2 NOTE MCP: create_shared_note
0 @N04@ SNOTE
1 TEXT Her memorial in Haworth church says she died on 15 September 1821 "in the 39th year of her age". That phrase means she was 38.
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 NOV 2026
3 TIME 18:10:38Z
2 NOTE MCP: create_shared_note
0 @N05@ SNOTE
1 TEXT Both girls went to the Clergy Daughters' School at Cowan Bridge in 1824. Both came home ill in 1825 and died within six weeks of each other. The manual treats these deaths plainly and uses a content note where a topic dwells on them.
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 NOV 2026
3 TIME 18:10:38Z
2 NOTE MCP: create_shared_note
0 @N06@ SNOTE
1 TEXT The sisters published as Currer, Ellis and Acton Bell. The pen names keep their initials. Charlotte explained the choice in her notice of her sisters in 1850.
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 NOV 2026
3 TIME 18:10:38Z
2 NOTE MCP: create_shared_note
0 @N07@ SNOTE
1 TEXT Her death was registered under her married name, Nicholls. A search of the death index under Brontë does not find her. See the negative search NS1 in the research threads file.
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 NOV 2026
3 TIME 18:10:44Z
2 NOTE MCP: create_shared_note
0 @N08@ SNOTE
1 TEXT The first memorial tablet gave his age at death as 30; the tablet that replaced it in 1858 says 31. The parish register records his burial on 28 September 1848.
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 NOV 2026
3 TIME 18:10:44Z
2 NOTE MCP: create_shared_note
0 @N09@ SNOTE
1 TEXT Her gravestone in Scarborough gives her age as 28. The replacement tablet in Haworth says 29. In 2013 the Brontë Society dedicated a plaque beside the grave that corrects the age.
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 NOV 2026
3 TIME 18:10:44Z
2 NOTE MCP: create_shared_note
0 @N10@ SNOTE
1 TEXT He was Patrick's curate from 1844 or 1845, married Charlotte in 1854, stayed with Patrick after her death, and went back to Banagher in 1861. He married his cousin Mary Anna Bell in 1864. They had no children. He died at Banagher on 2 December 1906 (C147).
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 NOV 2026
3 TIME 18:10:44Z
2 NOTE MCP: create_shared_note
0 @N16@ SNOTE
1 TEXT Two couples married in the same service at Guiseley on 29 December 1812: Patrick and Maria, and Maria's cousin Jane Fennell and William Morgan. Each bridegroom officiated at the other's wedding, because the brides' father and uncle, Mr Fennell, was giving both away. On the same day Maria's sister Charlotte married Joseph Branwell in Penzance.
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 NOV 2026
3 TIME 18:10:44Z
2 NOTE MCP: create_shared_note
0 @N17@ SNOTE
1 TEXT Charlotte's will, made in February 1855, left everything to her husband and provided for a child in case she "leave issue". She died six weeks later. No child is recorded.
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 NOV 2026
3 TIME 18:10:44Z
2 NOTE MCP: create_shared_note
0 @N18@ SNOTE
1 TEXT Portrait of Patrick Brontë, before 1861. The artist is unknown. It is public domain in the United States, and assumed public domain in the United Kingdom from the artist's age. From Wikimedia Commons, where the licence is stated. The picture is small, 291 by 378 pixels, which is low for print.
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 NOV 2026
3 TIME 18:13:32Z
2 NOTE MCP: create_shared_note
0 @N11@ _RNOT
1 TEXT Question: What was Patrick's surname at birth, and when did it become Brontë?
2 CONT
2 CONT Conclusion: The Irish register spells it Brunty; Prunty and Branty also occur; he signed Brontë from his curacies onward. Which spelling the family used is open. Confidence in the change itself: high.
1 _STAT in progress
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 NOV 2026
3 TIME 18:12:43Z
2 NOTE MCP: create_research_note
0 @N12@ _RNOT
1 TEXT Question: Where in County Down was Patrick born?
2 CONT
2 CONT Conclusion: Two sources name the townland Emdale; two name the parish Ahaderg; two say only County Down. We record Emdale with moderate confidence and leave the parish open.
1 _STAT in progress
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 NOV 2026
3 TIME 18:13:00Z
2 NOTE MCP: create_research_note
0 @N13@ _RNOT
1 TEXT Question: Why do the memorials give different ages for Branwell, Emily and Anne?
2 CONT
2 CONT Conclusion: The first tablet understates each age by one year, and Anne's gravestone by one. The baptism dates support the replacement tablet. Concluded: 31, 30 and 29. Confidence: high.
1 _STAT resolved
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 NOV 2026
3 TIME 18:13:00Z
2 NOTE MCP: create_research_note
0 @N14@ _RNOT
1 TEXT Question: In which year were Maria, Elizabeth and Anne born?
2 CONT
2 CONT Conclusion: Published works differ by a year for each. The baptisms and the memorial ages bound the years but do not settle them. Open until the registers are read.
1 _STAT open
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 NOV 2026
3 TIME 18:13:00Z
2 NOTE MCP: create_research_note
0 @N15@ _RNOT
1 TEXT Question: Why does the 1841 census give Patrick's age as 60?
2 CONT
2 CONT Conclusion: The 1841 census rounded adults' ages down to a multiple of five, so 60 is what the rules required for a man of 64. Open until the image is read: our only reading of the return is second-hand.
1 _STAT open
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 NOV 2026
3 TIME 18:13:00Z
2 NOTE MCP: create_research_note
0 @NS1@ _RNOT
1 TEXT Search: Charlotte's death in 1855, under the surname Brontë.
2 CONT
2 CONT Where we looked: the index of deaths for England and Wales, on FreeBMD, for Bronte, 1837 to 1861.
2 CONT
2 CONT Result: Not found under Brontë. Found under Nicholls, Keighley, June quarter 1855, volume 9a, page 135.
2 CONT
2 CONT What it tells us: A woman's death is indexed under her married name. Search every name a person used.
1 _STAT resolved
1 CHAN
2 DATE 17 NOV 2026
3 TIME 18:13:00Z
2 NOTE MCP: create_research_note
